Wait, perhaps an alternative way:

Actually, the dihedral angle \(\alpha\) can be related to the normals. The angle between the normals is either \(\alpha\) or \(\pi - \alpha\), so:
\[
\cos \alpha = \frac{ \vec{N}_{12} \cdot \vec{N}_{13} }{ |\vec{N}_{12}| |\vec{N}_{13}| }
\]
But we can also relate the scalar triple product to the areas and the sine of the dihedral angle.

Recall that the volume \( V = \text{Area}_{12} \cdot h_{3} \), where \( h_3 \) is the component of \(\vec{n}_3\) perpendicular to the plane 12, which is precisely the definition tied to the sine of the dihedral angle.

In more detail: the component of \(\vec{n}_3\) perpendicular to plane 12 (normal to \(\vec{N}_{12}\)) is \( |\vec{n}_3| \sin \alpha \), so the volume is also:
